@end menu

@node Fountain Canonical AST Mapping
@section Canonical AST Node Implementation

All Fountain language constructs map to canonical AST nodes per the frontend
conformance specification. The following summarizes the mappings:

@subsection Assignment and Arithmetic

Assignment uses @code{:move} nodes:
@example
SET $var TO expression → (:move :from expression :to var)
@end example

Arithmetic operations emit canonical binary operator nodes:
@example
SUM OF e1, e2           → (:add :from e1 :to e2 :giving nil)
DIFFERENCE OF e1, e2    → (:subtract :minuend e1 :subtrahend e2 :giving nil)
PRODUCT OF e1, e2       → (:multiply :multiplicand e1 :multiplier e2 :giving nil)
QUOTIENT OF e1, e2      → (:divide :numerator e1 :denominator e2 :giving nil)
@end example

Infix arithmetic also maps to canonical nodes:
@example
e1 + e2 → (:add :from e1 :to e2 :giving nil)
e1 - e2 → (:subtract :minuend e1 :subtrahend e2 :giving nil)
e1 * e2 → (:multiply :multiplicand e1 :multiplier e2 :giving nil)
e1 / e2 → (:divide :numerator e1 :denominator e2 :giving nil)
@end example

@subsection Null and Self References

Null value and self-reference literals are mapped to canonical representations:
@example
NULL              → :null
SELF              → :self
NULLIFY $var      → (:move :from :null :to var)
$var IS NULL      → (:null var)
$var IS NOT NULL  → (:not-null var)
@end example

@node Fountain Arithmetic Syntax
@section Arithmetic Word Syntax

Fountain provides English-language arithmetic operators that emit canonical
binary expression nodes:

@subsection Sum Operation
@example
SUM OF expr1, expr2
  → (:add :from expr1 :to expr2 :giving nil)
@end example

@subsection Difference Operation
@example
DIFFERENCE OF expr1, expr2
  → (:subtract :minuend expr1 :subtrahend expr2 :giving nil)
@end example

@subsection Product Operation
@example
PRODUCT OF expr1, expr2
  → (:multiply :multiplicand expr1 :multiplier expr2 :giving nil)
@end example

@subsection Quotient Operation
@example
QUOTIENT OF expr1, expr2
  → (:divide :numerator expr1 :denominator expr2 :giving nil)
@end example

@subsection Shift Operations
Bit shift operations use canonical shift nodes:
@example
SHIFT LEFT OF expr
  → (:shift-left expr count)
SHIFT RIGHT OF expr
  → (:shift-right expr count)
@end example

@node Fountain Control Flow
@section Control Flow Statements

@subsection WHEN Conditional
WHEN statements map to @code{:if} nodes:
@example
WHEN condition THEN
  statement1
  statement2
@end example
Maps to:
@example
(:if :condition condition
     :then (statement1 statement2)
     :else nil)
@end example

@subsection UNLESS Conditional
UNLESS statements are conditionals with negated conditions:
@example
UNLESS condition THEN
  statement1
@end example
Maps to:
@example
(:if :condition (:not condition)
     :then (statement1)
     :else nil)
@end example

@subsection REPEAT Loop
REPEAT...TIMES constructs map to @code{:perform} nodes:
@example
REPEAT 10 TIMES
  statement1
  statement2
@end example
Maps to:
@example
(:perform :procedure nil
          :times 10
          :body (statement1 statement2))
@end example

@subsection COND Multi-way Dispatch
COND statements map to @code{:evaluate} nodes with :when/:when-other clauses:
@example
COND
  WHEN condition1
    statement1
  WHEN condition2
    statement2
  ELSE
    statement3
@end example
Maps to:
@example
(:evaluate :clauses
  ((:when :condition condition1 :statements (statement1))
   (:when :condition condition2 :statements (statement2))
   (:when-other :statements (statement3))))
@end example

@node Fountain String Operations
@section String and Memory Operations

@subsection String Inspection

String inspection functions emit @code{:inspect} nodes:

@subsubheading LENGTH OF
@example
LENGTH OF string
  → (:inspect :length string)
@end example

@subsubheading CHARACTER AT
@example
CHARACTER AT index OF string
  → (:inspect :character string index)
@end example

@subsubheading POSITION OF
@example
POSITION OF substring IN string
  → (:inspect :position string substring)
@end example

@subsubheading COUNT OF
@example
COUNT OF substring IN string
  → (:inspect :count string substring)
@end example

@subsection Array Subscripting
Array access uses FROM...ELEMENT syntax mapping to @code{:subscript}:
@example
FROM $array ELEMENT $index
  → (:subscript array index)
@end example

@subsection Reference Modification
Substring operations map to @code{:refmod}:
@example
SUBSTRING OF $str FROM start TO end
  → (:refmod :base str :start start :length end)
@end example

@subsection Address-of Operation
Address-of operations produce canonical nodes:
@example
ADDRESS OF $var
  → (:address-of var)
@end example

@subsection Block Copy
Block copy operations produce canonical @code{:string-blt} nodes:
@example
BLOCK COPY OF $src TO $dest
  → (:string-blt :source src :dest dest)
@end example

@node Fountain Null and Self
@section Null and Self Reference Support

@subsection NULL Literal
The NULL literal represents a null pointer or uninitialized value:
@example
SET $ptr TO NULL
  → (:move :from :null :to ptr)
@end example

@subsection SELF Reference
The SELF keyword refers to the current object or scope:
@example
SET $obj TO SELF
  → (:move :from :self :to obj)
@end example

@subsection Null Tests
Fountain supports two forms of null tests that map to canonical test nodes:

@subsubheading IS NULL Test
@example
IF $var IS NULL
  → (:if :condition (:null var) ...)
@end example

@subsubheading IS NOT NULL Test
@example
IF $var IS NOT NULL
  → (:if :condition (:not-null var) ...)
@end example

@subsection Nullification
The NULLIFY operation sets a variable to null:
@example
NULLIFY $var
  → (:move :from :null :to var)
@end example

@section Number Literal Formats

The Fountain frontend lexer supports a variety of numeric literal formats:

@subsection Decimal Literals
Decimal integers and floating-point numbers are supported:
@itemize
@item @code{42} — integer literal
@item @code{3.14} — floating-point literal
@end itemize

@subsection Fractional Literals
Simple fractions are supported and parsed:
@itemize
@item @code{3/4} — fractional literal (three-quarters)
@item @code{1/2} — half
@end itemize

@subsection Binary Literals
Binary integers are supported using the @code{0b} prefix:
@itemize
@item @code{0b1010} — equals 10 (decimal)
@item @code{0b1111} — equals 15 (decimal)
@end itemize

@subsection Octal Literals
Octal integers are supported using the @code{0o} prefix:
@itemize
@item @code{0o10} — equals 8 (decimal)
@item @code{0o77} — equals 63 (decimal)
@end itemize

@subsection Hexadecimal Literals
Hexadecimal integers are supported using the @code{0x} prefix:
@itemize
@item @code{0xFF} — equals 255 (decimal)
@item @code{0x100} — equals 256 (decimal)
@item @code{0xDEADBEEF} — large hex value
@end itemize

@section Identifier Normalization

The front-end converts identifiers to the canonical PascalCase form before AST production. This ensures consistent naming regardless of input format:

@subsection Actor Names
Actor names are normalized to PascalCase:
@itemize
@item Input: @code{hero-man} → Output: @code{HeroMan}
@item Input: @code{PRINCESS_OF_DUNE} → Output: @code{PrincessOfDune}
@item Input: @code{NPC-with-dashes} → Output: @code{NpcWithDashes}
@end itemize

Whitespace and separators (hyphens, underscores) are removed and words are capitalized before storage in the AST.

@subsection Variable Names
Variables (prefixed with @code{$}) are similarly normalized:
@itemize
@item Input: @code{$hero_experience} → Output: @code{$HeroExperience}
@item Input: @code{$current-level} → Output: @code{$CurrentLevel}
@end itemize

This normalization ensures that Fountain scripts maintain consistent variable references regardless of stylistic variations in the source.
